  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through this Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Isolated showers and thunderstorms are \ncurrently moving across portions of east-central Nevada this \nmorning. Remote observation platforms are showing light rainfall\namounts as a result. \n\nFor today, upper level ridging has shifted east and will be over\nthe southern United States this afternoon. An upper trough will be\nover northeast Washington State, extending southwest across\nCalifornia. The resulting southwest flow has pushed in some drier\nair. Precipitable water values are around 0.25 to 0.40 inches \nover much of the forecast area, with greater than 0.50 inches \nacross northeast Nye and the eastern half of White Pine county. \nHave kept isolated to scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms\nover northeast Nye and White Pine counties today as a result. Not\nmuch in the way of rainfall accumulations are expected. High \ntemperatures this afternoon will be in the 70s and low 80s as a \nresult. Winds will generally be light with occasional gusts to 20 \nmph. Any convective activity is expected to dissipate by the early\nevening with mostly clear skies tonight with lows in the 40s and \n50s.\n\nThe aforementioned upper trough over California is forecast to\nshift eastward across Nevada Monday. Precipitable water values\nwill be on the increase during the late morning and afternoon with\nforecast amounts at or greater than two-thirds of an inch. Areal\ncoverage for isolated to scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorm underwent a westward expansion Monday versus Sunday.\nStorms could produce brief periods of moderate rainfall though\nstorm motions will be quite fast at 15 to 30 knots over east-\ncentral Nevada. Temperatures will remain near normal levels with\nreadings in the 70s and 80s. Afternoon breezes with gusts to 25\nmph will be limited to those locales along and south of the US\nHighway 50 corridor. Look for the convective activity to wane\nMonday night into Tuesday from west to east with lows in the 40s\nfor the most part.\n\nSouthwest flow continues over northern and central Nevada Tuesday\nwith isolated shower activity confined to basically the eastern \nhalves of Elko and White Pine counties. It will be breezy in the\nafternoon with gusts to 25 mph. Highs will be in the 70s and 80s\nwith overnight lows in the 40s.\n\nThe area may see some drier weather during the mid-week from\nWednesday through roughly Saturday with afternoon highs remaining\nnear normal with readings in the 70s and 80s and lows in the 40s.\nWinds will be breezy each afternoon with gusts of 20 to 25 mph. \nOvernight lows will be in the 40s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Near term gridded forecast was \nmodified to massage PoPs, Wx, and winds to reflect the possibility\nof showers and thunderstorms today and Monday. Confidence improves\nto moderate for precipitation chances Monday afternoon, though is\nlow with respect to amounts and location of convective activity.\nConfidence is also low at this time regarding the mid-week break\nin weather.",
